<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Which directories the FileProvider may hand out, and nothing more.

    The single entry is `<cacheDir>/updates/`. The in-app updater can put the
    downloaded APK there itself, and when it does not — the Dart side resolves
    its own download folder, which on Android is the app's *persistent*
    directory — `AppChannel.installApk` copies the file into this directory
    before minting the Uri. Either way the installer, which runs in another
    process and cannot open our private files, is lent exactly one directory:
    a cache directory needs no permission, is not backed up, and holding a
    throwaway APK there is what the cache is for.

    Three roots that were deliberately not added:

      · `root-path` — would expose the entire app-private tree (`databases/`
        included). It is the shortest way to make FileProvider "just work" and
        the fastest way to fail a Play review and to leak the word database to
        anything that can call the provider.
      · `files-path` — the tempting shortcut once the download lands in the
        persistent directory, but it would hand out that whole tree (exports,
        `lexumo.json`, the sync database) for the sake of one APK. Copying the
        APK into the cache instead keeps the exposed surface at one directory.
      · `external-path` — on API 29+ scoped storage makes the app's external
        directory a worse place to write than the cache, and pre-29 it meant
        asking for storage permissions. The updater should not need any.

    A path not listed here makes `FileProvider.getUriForFile` throw. The Kotlin
    side treats that as "stage it where the provider can reach it", and answers
    `noHandler` if even that fails — a wrong path fails loudly at the boundary
    instead of silently producing a Uri the installer cannot open.
-->
<paths>
    <cache-path name="updates" path="updates/"/>
</paths>
